---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/sections/Hero.astro';
/* Showcase (Nerd Apply) is out of the scene for now. The component is
   untouched in src/components/sections/. Putting it back is this import, the
   pixel field and `data-scene-next` layer below, and hero.ts already resumes
   the hero → Showcase phase when `[data-scene-next]` is in the page. */
import Overclock from '../components/sections/Overclock.astro';
/* Bedford and CELPIP are out of the scene until their case studies exist.
   A chapter that hands over to a page nobody can read yet is a promise the
   site cannot keep. The components are untouched in
   src/components/sections/; putting them back is this import, their two
   blocks below, and their phases in hero.ts. */
/* Credibility is out of the page for now. The component is untouched in
   src/components/sections/, and src/scripts/credibility.ts already returns a
   no-op when it cannot find `[data-cred]`, so nothing else had to change to
   take it out — except the exit field's colour and the trigger note below,
   both of which named it explicitly. */
import WorkCategories from '../components/sections/WorkCategories.astro';
import TwoWays from '../components/sections/TwoWays.astro';
import Clients from '../components/sections/Clients.astro';
import Testimonials from '../components/sections/Testimonials.astro';
import TestimonialsFloat from '../components/sections/TestimonialsFloat.astro';
import TestimonialsSphere from '../components/sections/TestimonialsSphere.astro';
/* Journal is out of the page for now. The component is untouched in
   src/components/sections/, and src/scripts/journal.ts already returns a
   no-op when it cannot find `[data-jr]`. Putting it back is this import and
   the block below. The nav "Blog" link comes back with it. */
import Footer from '../components/layout/Footer.astro';
---

<Base>
  {/*
    Two chapters, one scroll scene — hero and Overclock. Showcase (Nerd Apply)
    is out for now; putting it back restores the middle beat. src/scripts/hero.ts
    switches this into pinned mode (layers stacked absolutely on top of each
    other) and drives every handover as phases of the same extended pin:
      hero → Overclock       pixel reveal, scattered, in Overclock's ground
                              colour — then the layer swaps in behind it
      Overclock → Our work   the same arrival: Overclock recedes, a cream
                              field scatters, then Our work fades in on it.
                              The pin ends as that finishes.
    Without JS — or with reduced motion — the chapters stay in normal flow and
    read top to bottom, no pin, no crossfades.
  */}
  {/* Out of the scene, and that is the point of it.

      The hero used to be the scene's first layer, which made its opening one
      more step in a stepped sequence: a scroll, and the chapter changed. The
      hero's own reveal is not a chapter change — it is one continuous thing
      the reader scrubs, so it has a pin and a scrubbed trigger of its own (see
      initHeroReveal in src/scripts/hero.ts) and the stepped scene begins after
      it, at Overclock. */}
  <Hero />

  {/* Overclock, and it is an ordinary section now.

      It used to be a stacked layer inside a pinned "scene" that also held the
      hero, with a gesture-stepper moving between them behind fields of
      scattered tiles. All of that is gone — see the note at the end of
      initHero in src/scripts/hero.ts. Overclock has a pinned scrub of its own
      instead (src/scripts/overclock.ts): it arrives small on black with its
      film already running, grows to fill the screen, and only then says
      anything. */}
  <Overclock />

  {/*
    After Overclock. It runs to about eight screens of its own.

    The handover into it is a crossfade now — Overclock fades out as this fades
    in — rather than the cream tile field the stepped scene used to scatter
    over it. See src/scripts/overclock.ts. What follows was written for that
    parks it over the viewport for that step. After the scene's pin it is
    ordinary page, scrolled at whatever rate the reader scrolls.

    One category at a time, full screen, from the Figma "Animation" frame: a
    sticky photograph per category with the copy scrolling in front of it. It
    holds nothing — the scene's pin is the last thing on this page that takes
    the scrollbar. The ground crossfade lives in
    src/scripts/workCategories.ts; the sticking is the stylesheet's.
  */}
  {/* The join between Overclock and Our work.

      A black panel that rises from the foot of the window as Our work arrives
      and then fades off it, leaving the reader already inside the section.
      Fixed, so it covers the window rather than a slice of the page, and
      pointer-transparent, so nothing under it stops being clickable while it
      is on.

      It lives here rather than inside either section because it belongs to
      neither: it has to cover the last of one and the first of the other, and
      an element inside a section cannot paint outside it. Driven by
      initWorkHandover in src/scripts/workCategories.ts, off Our work's own
      position on the page.

      `data-nav-over="dark"` for the same reason every dark section carries it:
      while the curtain is up it is what the nav's capsule is over, and the
      capsule has to be glass against it rather than ink. The watcher reads
      visibility and opacity, so the curtain stops counting the moment it has
      faded. */}
  <div class="handover" data-handover data-nav-over="dark" aria-hidden="true"></div>

  <WorkCategories />

  {/*
    Same plain-scroll treatment as the section above it, which it used to be.
    Its one-shot entrance lives in src/scripts/twoWays.ts.
  */}
  <TwoWays />

  {/*
    A band rather than a chapter — ruled top and bottom, one row tall, and the
    only thing on the page that moves without being scrolled. It introduces the
    section under it, so the two are adjacent on purpose. No script: the loop is
    a CSS animation over a list rendered twice.
  */}
  <Clients />

  {/*
    Chapter 8, three times. The "Testimonial section" axis in
    src/config/variations.ts picks between them and each stylesheet
    display:none's itself when it is not the pick, so only one is ever on the
    page — all three are rendered because the choice is the reader's, made in
    the browser, and this site is built once.

    Film wall (the default): pins itself for a few screens — one film alone,
    then the title over it, then a wall of testimonials scrolling up under the
    title. src/scripts/testimonials.ts.

    Scrolled scatter: nothing is pinned. A tall section with the title stuck
    in the middle of it and six tiles at the frame's own positions down it,
    all of it moved by the scroll itself. It ends on the footer's photograph,
    which opens out from a card into the footer's own band —
    src/scripts/testimonialsFloat.ts for the entrances, and
    src/scripts/founderQuote.ts for the opening, because that picture belongs
    to the footer.

    Orbit grid: the founders as tiles on a sphere that turns by itself and
    under a drag. Pressing one does not open a dialog over it — that tile
    flies out of the rotation into the read and the quote arrives beside it,
    with the arrows and the keyboard walking between founders from there.
    src/scripts/testimonialsSphere.ts.

    Without JS, or under reduced motion, any of them is a title over a grid of
    films in ordinary flow.
  */}
  <Testimonials />
  <TestimonialsFloat />
  <TestimonialsSphere />

  {/* Closing block, in two sections: the founder quote on its photograph,
      then the ink band. See Footer.astro. */}
  <Footer />
</Base>

<style>
  /* The handover curtain. Parked below the window and invisible until the
     script picks it up — so a page without JS never has a black panel in it,
     and one with JS starts with it off screen.

     Under the header and over everything else: the nav band sits at 90 and
     above, and it stays legible across the join. */
  .handover {
    position: fixed;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 80;
    background: var(--color-bg-work);
    pointer-events: none;
    transform: translate3d(0, 100%, 0);
    visibility: hidden;
  }

  /* The scene's stylesheet is gone with the scene.

     What stood here dressed a pinned, gesture-stepped sequence: stacked
     full-viewport chapter layers, two grids of tiles for the handovers between
     them, a zero-height marker for the pin's release, and the rules that hid
     all of it once the sequence was spent. None of those elements are in the
     page any more — the hero and Overclock each pin and scrub on their own,
     and everything between them is ordinary scrolling page. */
</style>
